Where AI Made Things Easier
Debugging serial monitor output was the biggest win. Every time the serial monitor showed an unexpected value or error, AI could look at the output and identify the root cause in seconds. What would have taken hours of searching forums took minutes. For example when the pressure sensor was reading -2.0000 kPa constantly, AI immediately recognized it as a floating ADC pin and suggested moving from A0 to A1 — confirmed and fixed in under 5 minutes.
Wiring tables saved a significant amount of time. Rather than hunting through datasheets and pinout diagrams, AI generated exact pin-by-pin connection tables for every component. This was especially helpful when connecting the Arduino Nano to the XIAO ESP32-C6 via serial — the exact pins, baud rate, and code were all provided together.
Rewriting firmware functions without breaking the rest of the code was something AI handled very well. When the NimBLE library needed to be replaced with the standard ESP32 BLE library, AI rewrote the entire BLE setup section while keeping all the sensor reading logic intact. A full library swap that could have taken a day was done in one session.
Building the dashboard was faster with AI than it would have been alone. The Web Bluetooth API has very specific syntax for scanning, connecting, and subscribing to BLE characteristics. AI wrote the connection logic, the JSON parser, the gauge animations, and the alert system — all working on the first attempt.

Where AI Made Things More Challenging
AI did not know about ESP32-C6 hardware limitations. The biggest time loss of the entire project came from AI confidently providing a bit-bang 1-Wire driver for the DS18B20 sensor that it said would work on the XIAO. It did not — the ESP32-C6 uses a RISC-V core where software timing for 1-Wire protocol fails. This was not discovered until after days of debugging scratchpad bytes showing all FF. AI had no knowledge of this chip-specific limitation and never warned about it. The fix — offloading the sensor to an Arduino Nano — was also an AI suggestion, but only after the problem was clearly identified.
Outdated library suggestions caused real setbacks. AI suggested NimBLE for BLE communication which is a widely used library. What it did not know was that NimBLE is invisible to iOS devices in certain configurations, and that newer versions had changed callback function signatures. Both issues caused compilation errors and hours of debugging before switching to the standard ESP32 BLE library resolved everything.
AI suggested functions that do not exist. analogSetAttenuation() was suggested for configuring the ADC — this function does not exist on the XIAO ESP32-C6 board package. The correct function is analogSetPinAttenuation(). Small mistakes like this are easy to make but they produce cryptic compiler errors that are frustrating to trace back to a single wrong function name.

